{
  "dom_id": "step-q224897",
  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "Factor the following quadratic expression.\n\n$20{b}^{2}-12b+1=(2b\\,-$\n{{field-1}}$)($\n{{field-2}}$b\\,-$\n{{field-3}}$)$",
  "worked_solution": "We must factor the quadratic expression\n\n$$\n20{b}^{2}-12b+1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find two numbers that \n\n- multiply to the product of the quadratic coefficient and the constant ($20\\times 1=20$), and\n\n- add to the linear coefficient ($-12$).\n\nSince the linear coefficient is negative and the product of the quadratic coefficient and constant is positive, we only need to consider the negative factor pairs of $20.$\n\nThe negative factor pairs of $20$ are\n\n$$\n(-1,-20),\\,(-2,-10),\\,(-4,-5).\n$$\n\nNotice the numbers in the factor pair $(-2,-10)$ add to $-12:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2)+(-10) & =-12\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we write the linear term as $-2b-10b,$ and factor the result by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}20{b}^{2}-12b+1 & = \\\\ 20{b}^{2}-2b-10b+1 & = \\\\ (20{b}^{2}-2b)-(10b-1) & = \\\\ 2b(10b-1)-(10b-1) & = \\\\ (2b-1)(10b-1) & \\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
